description:
In case you were wondering (or were confused by last lessons "hint" challenge), you can call functions with `await` to immediatly `await` the returned promise, because it's just a value, and puting a value in a variable doesn't change that it's still a value. It's the same value right after call, it doesn't need to cool off like cookies.

---
`await` can be used instead of `.then()`. `async` simplifies or replaces the entire `new Promise` nonsense when you use it. You make a function `async` by putting `async` before `function`.
> You can only use `await` in the top level and async functions.

---
## new Promise
```ts
function doSomthing(): Promise<number> {
  return new Promise<number>((resolve) => {
    doSomthingWithServer().then((data) => {
      resolve(data);
    }
  });
}
```
> You can also make Async big arrow functions by putting async before the big arrow func def, or pass a normal function to a function-taking-function, you can do `function (args) {},` as the argument, and make that `async`.
```ts
async function doSomthing() {
return /* the await is optional when returning from a async function */ doSomthingWithServer();
}
```

---
## Assignment
Make the long `updateVideo` function `async` to avoid `new Promise`. (You're gonna need to wrap the setTimeout in a awaited promise, because you can't return values returned in a setTimeout.)

starterCode:
function updateVideo(currentStatus: string, timeUploaded: number, videoName: string): Promise<string> {
  return new Promise<string>((resolve, reject) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
      if (currentStatus === "In draft") {
        resolve(`Succesfully updated video ${videoName}`);
      } else if (currentStatus === "Published" && timeUploaded < 100) {
        reject(`Video ${videoName} hasn't been uploaded long enough to update.`);
      } else {
        resolve(`Published video ${videoName} updated.`);
      }
    }, 500);
  });
}
// Make me async!

runHarness:
console.log(`Test 1:
Is updateVideo async?
${Object.prototype.toString.call(updateVideo).includes('Async') ? 'Pass' : 'Fail'}`);
const value = await updateVideo("In draft", 0, "Tester");
console.log(`Test 2:
Does it still work?
${value == 'Succesfully updated video Tester' ? 'Pass' : 'Fail'}`);
